      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Case report."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To report the first case of 4-level lumbar spondylolysis in the literature."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "Although there are quite a few reported cases of 2- or 3-level spondylolysis, none reported on 4-level spondylolysis. Management guidelines have not been suggested in literature."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A 48-year-old female presented with long-standing severe spinal instability, low back pain, and bilateral neurogenic claudication. Management of the patient with 4-level spondylolysis, varied clinical profile of each of the levels with respect to clinical symptoms and treatment based on the usefulness of pars block is described."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Two levels were treated with intervertebral fusions, 1 level with pars repair, and conservative treatment of the last level pars lysis yielded in good clinicoradiological outcome in our patient."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Success of management of multiple lyses depends on the choice of appropriate treatment for each level separately. Pars block is a good invasive investigation to detect the symptomatic levels in a complex situation."
        }
      ]},
